If we draw two segments in a circle with a radius of 1 with an angle of $s$ between them, $s$ is equal to the length of a segment that is tangent to the larger circle and takes the same time to go around the circumference as the seconds hand takes to go once.  It follows that an angle of $s$ corresponds to an arc that has a length of $s$ or that has an area of $s$.  It also follows that if the angle is greater than $1$, then a segment with length of $s$ will be formed.

First, the maximum area that can be formed with two sides of 1 is 1/2, so $\theta = 1/2$.  The area that can be formed with an angle greater than 1 is greater than $1/2$, because more than half the angle can be used to form a segment.

Now, since the maximum length of a side is 1, and the second longest length is less than $4/5$ (draw the right angle with one side of 1, connect the right angle to the center, and / or draw the circle, which has an area of $\pi$, and a smaller circle of radius off 1/5 is $\pi/5^2$).  Since $1 > 4/5$ and $1 > \pi/5^2$ $1 > \pi/5^2+p \geq 4/5$. It follows that an obtuse triangle can be formed with $p = (1-4/5)/2 = 1/10$, and $4p = \boxed{4/10} = \boxed{2/5}$. </think> <answer> 2/5 </answer>

