draw(rightanglemark(X,W,Y,25));
label("$12$",(Y+Z)/2,S);
label("$A$", (0,0), S);
[/asy]

Since $\tan W = 3$, \[\tan W = \frac{WX}{WZ} = \frac{[WXZ]}{12} = 3,\]so the area $[WXZ]$ of $\triangle WXZ$ is $3 \cdot 12 = 36$. $\triangle WXZ$ also has hypotenuse $WX,$ and from the tangent, $\tan W = \frac{\text{opposite side of }W}{\text{adjacent side of }W} =\frac{WX}{12} = 3,$ so $WX = 36$.

Since $\tan Z = 1.5$, the area $[ZWX]$ of $\triangle ZWX$ is \[\tan Z = \frac{ZWX}{WZ}= \frac{ZWX}{36}=.5,\]so the area $ZWX]$ of $\triangle ZWX$ equals $.5\cdot 36 = 18$. These two triangles contained the top and bottom bases of trapezoid $WXYZ$; plus the height of the trapezoid is $YW = 12 - \frac{[WZY]}{12} = 12 -3 = 9$.

Therefore, by the formula for the area of a trapezoid, \[[WXYZ] = [ZWX] + [WZY] + [WXZ] = 18 + 36 + 36 = \boxed{90}.\] </think> <answer> 90 </answer>

